=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket's premise is entirely false. The user asserts that
spine_router_0_0 has "crashed" and that pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) cannot reach
pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2), but direct measurement contradicts both claims.

On spine_router_0_0, all five FRR daemons (watchfrr, zebra, mgmtd, bgpd,
staticd) are actively running with normal RSS and uptime. Its BGP session
table shows all three eBGP peers Established: the super_spine (AS 65000),
leaf_router_0_0 (AS 65200), and leaf_router_0_1 (AS 65201), each exchanging
the expected prefix counts. The same healthy picture holds on spine_router_0_1
and all leaf routers — every BGP session across the five-node CLOS fabric is
up, all 14 peerings in Established state.

Routing tables on both leaf routers are complete: leaf_router_0_0 carries a
BGP-learned route to 10.0.1.0/24 via spine_router_0_0 (172.16.0.4, eth0),
and leaf_router_0_1 carries a BGP-learned route to 10.0.0.0/24 via the same
spine. A live ping from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 returns 4/4 packets with 0% loss
and sub-2 ms RTT, confirming full end-to-end data-plane connectivity.

Root cause: there is no fault — the ticket is a false alarm based on an
incorrect premise about spine_router_0_0 being down.

No corrective action is needed. The operator should be advised that the
CLOS fabric is healthy, spine_router_0_0 is fully operational, and
inter-rack traffic is flowing normally.
=== END ===
